    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">26-2009.00236v2_rag::chunk_005</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DAL algorithms, and new basic learned models. This makes the application of DAL to downstream tasks</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">While deep learning (DL) is data-hungry and usually relies on extensive labeled data to deliver good performance, Active Learning (AL) reduces labeling costs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This makes the application of DAL to downstream tasks, and designing new DAL algorithms becomes easier.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">training of AL query samples on the DL model. Although the query strategies in DeepAL are rich and complex</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Deep Active Learning (DeepAL), an approach combining DL and AL to maximize task performance while minimizing</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">deep active learning (DAL) starts with a random initialization.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Active learning (AL) combines data labeling and model training to minimize the labeling cost</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This section provides an overview of highly-cited DAL methods in recent years, including the perspectives of querying strategies and techniques for enhancing DAL methods.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Better and more universal enhancement methods are needed in DAL.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">existing deep active learning methods are usually designed based on heuristic criteria</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Active Learning (AL) reduces labeling costs by select</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">feature representations are dynamically changing during DAL processes</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Active learning (AL) is a label-ecient technique for training deep models when only a limited labeled set is available and the manual annotation is expensive.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">to as DeepAL, was proposed by considering the complementary advantages of the two methods</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">transited to deep neural networks. Gal et al. (2017) used Monte-Carlo (MC) Dropout (Gal and Ghahramani, 2016) to generate posterior samples of the neural network output</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">application of DAL to downstream tasks, and designing new DAL algorithms becomes easier.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A Survey of Deep Active Learning 5 It is different from DL by using manual or</hasEvidenceText>
